// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Enums
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// §2 and §3 of the master architecture use twelve enum types by name in their
// DDL. FIVE of them state their values; SEVEN do not appear anywhere in the
// document except as a column type. Those seven are chosen here, and each one
// carries the reasoning, because an enum is the one thing in this baseline that
// is expensive to get wrong in BOTH directions:
//
//   - ADDING a value later is cheap. `ALTER TYPE … ADD VALUE` is a catalogue
//     write, and since Postgres 12 it may run inside a transaction provided the
//     new value is not used in the same one.
//   - REORDERING or REMOVING one is not. Label order is what `ORDER BY` on an
//     enum column sorts by, so a reorder silently changes every query that
//     sorts on it, and a removal requires rewriting every table that uses the
//     type.
//
// So each of the seven is deliberately MINIMAL — only values the document
// actually names or a constraint actually forces — and later phases are
// expected to append. A short enum that grows is a correct enum; a long one
// guessed today is a reorder waiting to happen.

// -- Stated by the document ---------------------------------------------------

/// Leader/06 record kind. Values stated verbatim at MASTER-ARCHITECTURE.md:60.
enum MarcRecordKind {
  bibliographic
  authority
  holdings
  classification

  @@map("marc_record_kind")
}

/// Which MARC dialect the record is in. Stated at MASTER-ARCHITECTURE.md:61.
/// `unimarc` is first-class from day one because ABEKT — the system most Greek
/// libraries are migrating off — is UNIMARC, and a schema that could only hold
/// MARC 21 would have to be migrated again at phase 50.
enum MarcSchema {
  marc21
  unimarc

  @@map("marc_schema")
}

/// Editorial state. Values stated at MASTER-ARCHITECTURE.md:62.
enum MarcRecordStatus {
  draft
  in_process
  complete
  suppressed
  deleted

  @@map("marc_record_status")
}

/// Call-number scheme. NOT invented: these are exactly the six schemes
/// `@libriant/shared/callnumber` implements a sort-key generator for
/// (packages/shared/src/callnumber/normalize.ts:49), and a seventh value here
/// would be a scheme whose `call_number_sort` nothing can compute.
enum CallNumberScheme {
  ddc
  lcc
  udc
  nlm
  alphanum
  local

  @@map("call_number_scheme")
}

/// Loan state.
///
/// The first four are FORCED, not chosen: `loans_closed_consistency`
/// (MASTER-ARCHITECTURE.md:402) is `(closed_at IS NULL) = (status IN
/// ('active','claims_returned','claims_never_borrowed','recalled'))`, which
/// names them as exactly the open set. `returned` and `lost` are the two closed
/// states the surrounding prose names.
///
/// `claims_returned` deliberately keeps the loan OPEN: the patron says they
/// brought it back and the shelf says otherwise, the item is unaccounted for,
/// and it must not be re-lendable while that is true.
enum LoanStatus {
  active
  recalled
  claims_returned
  claims_never_borrowed
  returned
  lost

  @@map("loan_status")
}

// -- Chosen here; the document names the type and never its values ------------

/// Who did it.
///
/// CHOSEN. 1.0's `AuditActorType` is (user, admin, system) and those three
/// carry over unchanged so the phase-19 copy-forward is a straight cast.
/// `device` is added because `change_events` and `sync_client_changes` both
/// carry a `device_id`, and from M8 an offline client writes rows on its own
/// authority — recording those as `system` would make the reconciliation report
/// unable to say which wand did what.
enum AuditActorKind {
  user
  admin
  system
  device

  @@map("audit_actor_kind")
}

/// What kind of place a branch is.
///
/// CHOSEN, minimally. `branch` is the default the document gives
/// (MASTER-ARCHITECTURE.md:191). `system` is the root of the hierarchy — the
/// library service itself, `parent_branch_id IS NULL` and `depth = 0` — and it
/// exists because ISIL and `marc_org_code` are properties of the SERVICE, not
/// of a reading room. `bookmobile` and `storage` are the two physical kinds a
/// Greek public library actually has that circulate differently (a bookmobile
/// is a pickup location on a timetable; closed storage is not a pickup location
/// at all). `virtual` holds electronic material that has no shelf, which the
/// `NOT NULL permanent_location_id` on `items` otherwise makes unrepresentable.
enum BranchKind {
  system
  branch
  bookmobile
  storage
  virtual

  @@map("branch_kind")
}

/// Where a copy is in the circulation cycle.
///
/// CHOSEN, and deliberately narrower than 1.0's `BookCopyStatus`. This is the
/// CIRCULATION state only: `not_for_loan_code`, `damaged_code`, `lost_code` and
/// `withdrawn_at` are separate columns on the same table by design, and the
/// generated `is_shelf_available` reads all five. Folding "damaged" back in as
/// a status would make an item both `damaged` and `damaged_code IS NULL`, and
/// the two answers would drift.
///
/// `in_transit` and `awaiting_pickup` are the two states the transit desk
/// (phase 21) and the hold shelf (phase 17) need and 1.0 has no way to express
/// — in 1.0 a copy moving between branches is simply `on_loan` to nobody.
enum ItemStatus {
  available
  on_loan
  in_transit
  awaiting_pickup
  in_process
  missing

  @@map("item_status")
}

/// Which channel produced a circulation event.
///
/// CHOSEN: one value per channel §5 of the master architecture gives a phase to
/// and that can create a loan — the staff desk (default), the public catalogue
/// (phase 33), a self-check unit over SIP2 (phase 61), a partner system over
/// NCIP (phase 62), the REST API (phase 56), the offline client replaying its
/// queue (phase 78) and kiosk mode (phase 81).
///
/// This is a STATISTICAL bucket, and it survives anonymisation: "how many
/// checkouts happened at the self-check?" is an ISO 2789 return element, and it
/// has to remain answerable after `patron_id` is nulled.
enum EventSource {
  desk
  opac
  sip2
  ncip
  api
  offline
  kiosk

  @@map("event_source")
}

/// What has happened to a charge.
///
/// CHOSEN: the first four mirror the four money columns on `fees` exactly
/// (`amount_cents` outstanding, then `paid_cents`, `waived_cents`,
/// `written_off_cents`), so the status is always derivable from the ledger and
/// can be asserted against it.
///
/// `cancelled` is separate from `waived` on purpose. Waiving is a decision to
/// forgive a debt that was correctly owed and is a reportable act a librarian
/// needs a permission for; cancelling is the correction of a charge that should
/// never have been raised. Collapsing them loses the distinction an auditor
/// asks about first.
enum FeeStatus {
  outstanding
  paid
  waived
  written_off
  cancelled

  @@map("fee_status")
}

/// How a record arrived.
///
/// CHOSEN: the three serializations `packages/marc` implements
/// (`iso2709.ts`, MARCXML, MARC-in-JSON) plus `manual` for a record typed into
/// the editor, which has no source bytes at all. This is what decides whether
/// `marc_record_contents.source_blob` can be non-NULL, so a value that is not a
/// byte format would be a lie about the fidelity promise.
enum MarcSourceFormat {
  iso2709
  marcxml
  marc_json
  manual

  @@map("marc_source_format")
}

/// Why a version row exists.
///
/// CHOSEN: one value per write path §6 gives a phase to. `edit` is the dual-mode
/// editor (28-29), `import` a migration adapter (35), `overlay` copy cataloguing
/// applying `marc_overlay_rules` (30), `batch` a batch edit (37), `merge` a
/// duplicate merge (39), `restore` a version restore (10), `delete` the
/// tombstone that `deletedRecord=persistent` promises OAI-PMH harvesters (59).
///
/// This is what the version list shows a cataloguer as the reason for a change,
/// so it is a user-facing vocabulary, not an implementation detail.
enum MarcChangeKind {
  create
  edit
  import
  overlay
  batch
  merge
  restore
  delete

  @@map("marc_change_kind")
}
